---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../components/Byline.astro";
import LegalNote from "../components/LegalNote.astro";
import IndexSeriesTable from "../components/IndexSeriesTable.astro";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const path = "/retailbi-firearm-sales-index";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "How to read the Index", path },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How they build the Index — in their words, then mine | FFL Intel"
  description="Gearfire’s monthly Firearm Sales Index is a register series. Same stores. New firearms. Subject to revision. That is a real thing. It is still last month."
  path={path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.tzu}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3">
      The Index
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white text-balance mb-4">
      How they build the Index — in their words, then mine
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">
      Same stores. New firearms. A percent versus last year. Subject to
      revision. I can live with that. I will not live with calling it the whole
      market.
    </p>
    <Byline dateLabel="August 13, 2026" read="8 min" />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(`${CANON_PATHS.tzu}?v=2`)}
        alt="Betsy Tzu — Art of Demand"
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full"
      />
      <figcaption class="px-4 py-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        Art of Demand — illustration, not a product shot. Last month’s
        sell-through is last season’s map.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif">
      <p>
        Betsy — night desk. Gearfire put the Index on Outdoor Wire on
        September 3, 2025. First print scheduled the fifth. Subscribers in
        October. Method, from the April 2026 file and every file like it: pull
        POS and ecommerce from the network, keep new firearms, keep only
        retailers who reported in both this window and last year’s, print the
        percent change. Openings and closings do not get to pose as demand.
        Used guns do not get to pose as new. That is a grown-up series.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seymour, at launch: the supply chain “deserves a measurement that
        isn’t clouded by irrelevant information, circumstantial influences,
        and unfounded assumptions.” He was talking about NICS. He was not
        talking about search. Those are different clouds.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        The public series
      </p>
      <IndexSeriesTable />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif">
      <p>
        Read it as a year, not a slide. August opened ugly. November was
        uglier. The December file printed the 2025 year at minus 12.1. The
        annual, on Tactical Wire January 29 and again on NSSF February 23,
        called the climate about 13 percent. January’s first print was minus
        6.2. February’s file moved January to minus 5.7. March printed minus
        11.9, year-to-date minus 8.2. Their 2026 outlook, on that NSSF note,
        was another seven to twelve percent down. March is how an outlook
        starts to look like a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        As of this filing — 13 August 2026 — Outdoor Wire’s last monthly is
        still the April 13 file. I will not invent the spring. Samples move.
        Believe the revision line.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I will not do is hypnotize myself. A same-store new-gun print is
        the register talking about last month. It is not NICS. It is not the
        used-gun aisle. It is not the sentence on a dealer site at 9:40 p.m.
        After you read it, the next file is still the ask. Comparison:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.fortress} class={linkClass} {...ext}>RetailBI vs Betsy</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href={LINKS.gseIndex} class={linkClass} {...ext}>GSE Index spoke</a>.
      </p>
      <p>That’s the file.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-10"><LegalNote fortress /></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
